# Workflow

## 1. Study the subject
- Open the user's attached image first when there is one. Otherwise search the user's exact subject name before
  broadening it. Keep the user's words separate from your interpretation; a related logo or similar name is not
  evidence that you have found the requested object. Resolve conflicting references before building.
- Name the build with `bricks name "<name>"` and choose a primary reference with `bricks reference <image-path>`.
  Render commands automatically reattach that image, so compare the current construction with the same target.
- Search again with other words for the other sides and the details you need ("Tower Bridge walkway wikimedia",
  "Tower Bridge from the river wikimedia"), download the best photos and look at each. A photo that shows the whole
  subject from 3/4, in daylight, is the best guide; choose one as your main reference. For a big building, find its
  floor plan too: the layout comes before any detail.
- Write `notes.md`: the request verbatim; verified observations separately from assumptions; each photo you follow,
  with its path, title and URL and what it shows; the signature
  features, each concrete and counted; the proportions read off the photos; the levels of the slice, bottom to top; a
  map of the footprint as rectangles (x, y, w, d) with heights in plates; the palette. Keep it current: it is what you
  will know about this build at the next request. Keep a short checklist of observable features and open defects.
  Once a reference contradicts your model, keep that defect open until a new render demonstrates its correction.
- Mirror the plan with `update_plan`: the signature features first, the setting and life after.

## 2. The first draft
- Write and run a compact first `build.py` for the subject's main masses, support and proportions. Use a few named
  steps and functions for repeated elements. Get a visible silhouette before planning decorative details.
- Compare that first render with the primary reference: silhouette, relative dimensions, number and location of
  major features. Correct these before adding scenery or texture. The photo's background is not automatically
  part of the user's request.

## 3. Grow and rebuild
- Each run adds or rebuilds whole features, each in its own named step, from the subject's signature features to the
  setting, then the life.
- After every run, write a critique in your message before the next edit:
  - problems: a rejected candidate did not replace the model. Fix its named lines before requesting another look;
    that look would still show the accepted version. Then fix every floating brick the run lists, by its script line;
  - defects found close up (see Looking): holes, joins, floating or cut parts, each with its place;
  - each signature feature: right, wrong (say what), or missing, against the reference photo;
  - the weakest part of the model now, and the change that makes it the strongest. Then make it.
  Fix the highest-priority open defect with one local edit and run in the same turn. Check its result before
  starting an unrelated improvement. If two attempts do not improve it, inspect the exact part dimensions and a
  close view, then change approach. Keep unresolved defects in notes; a successful tool exit does not close them.
- The four views show the whole; details hide in them. Look closely at a feature you are judging, from the angle of its
  reference photo, with `bricks look` and a camera.

## 4. Detail passes
- When every feature stands, pass over the model level by level, then side by side, as a visitor would: texture and
  depth where the subject has them, life where it would really be, the foreground, the skyline, the back view. Close
  views (`--zoom 3` or more, at eye level) show what a visitor would see there.
- Compare with the photos first, then the showcase: where does yours read less like the subject? That is the next
  pass.
- A pass on a part you have no close photo of starts with a `web_search` for it.

## 5. Finish
- Answer when the last candidate was accepted and the reference checklist is satisfied by the latest renders.
  Describe unresolved limitations honestly, including details the available tools cannot represent. Do not polish
  scenery while a major likeness defect remains. The budget is a ceiling, not a target or a minimum number of turns.
- Before `answer`, update `notes.md` with what you built and remaining limitations. Your answer should state the
  result and material limitations honestly; do not claim mechanical verification or perfect reference fidelity.

## Action chains

```
# the first step
shell: bricks name "Tower Bridge"
web_search: Tower Bridge London wikimedia
view_image: showcase/london.png

# keep the best photos, then select the main reference
shell: curl -sLA Mozilla/5.0 -o reference-1.jpg "URL" && curl -sLA Mozilla/5.0 -o reference-2.jpg "URL"; file reference-*
shell: bricks reference reference-1.jpg

# a closer look at a detail
web_search: Tower Bridge high-level walkway wikimedia

# every change is an edit and its run, in one step
search_replace: build.py (the step to change)
shell: bricks run

# a finding goes into the notes in the same step as the work it led to
search_replace: notes.md (the defect found, the fix chosen)
search_replace: build.py (the fix)
shell: bricks run

# compare against the main photo after a run, from the photo's angle, in one result
shell: bricks look --angle 320 --elevation 10 --zoom 2.5 --at 16 14 12

# the closest showcase beside your main photo, before the draft
shell: echo "@@attach showcase/london.png"; echo "@@attach reference-1.jpg"

# the back at eye level, where plain walls and holes hide
shell: bricks look --angle 180 --elevation 5 --zoom 2

# the whole model, and a close-up of the part you just changed, alone
shell: bricks run && bricks look 10 4 0 20 12 30

# a weak part: replace its whole function with a new design, then run
write_file: build.py (the script with the new function)
shell: bricks run

# learn a technique from a showcase
shell: grep -n 'def plane\|def lamp\|def bench' showcase/paris.py
read_file: showcase/paris.py (the lines of that function)
```

